# Low-Level Design (LLD) Interview Guidelines

## 1. Clarify Requirements
- Ask clarifying questions to nail down functional and non-functional requirements.
- Identify use cases, constraints (performance, concurrency, scalability), and failure scenarios.

## 2. Identify Core Entities and Relationships
- List domain objects (entities, value objects, enums).
- Define associations (composition, aggregation, inheritance).
- Sketch a UML class diagram or ASCII/Markdown diagram.

## 3. Apply SOLID Principles
- Single Responsibility: each class has one reason to change.
- Open/Closed: design modules open for extension, closed for modification.
- Liskov Substitution: subtypes should be substitutable for their base types.
- Interface Segregation: clients should not depend on unused interfaces.
- Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concretions.

## 4. Separation of Concerns & Layering
- Split responsibilities into layers (presentation, service/business, repository/persistence).
- Decouple UI, domain logic, and data access.
- Use interfaces, factories, or dependency injection for wiring.

## 5. Thread Safety & Concurrency
- Identify shared mutable state and protect via synchronization or concurrent collections.
- Minimize critical sections; prefer immutability where possible.
- Consider lock granularity and deadlock avoidance.

## 6. Spot Assignment & Resource Management
- Model resources (e.g., parking spots) as objects with unique IDs, states, and lifecycle methods.
- Provide allocate() and release() operations; track availability per instance, not only counts.

## 7. Error Handling & Validation
- Use custom exceptions for domain errors.
- Validate inputs at boundaries; fail fast on invalid state.
- Provide meaningful error messages and recovery options.

## 8. Design Patterns
- Use Strategy, Factory, Singleton, Observer, etc., where they fit recurring problems.
- Avoid overusing patterns; prefer simple solutions first.

## 9. Scalability & Performance
- Estimate concurrency level and throughput requirements.
- Design for horizontal scaling (stateless services, sharding, caching).
- Avoid bottlenecks in critical paths.

## 10. Persistence & Transactions
- Abstract persistence behind repositories or DAOs.
- Manage transactions consistently (unit of work, rollback on failure).
- Consider eventual consistency vs. strong consistency.

## 11. Testing & Extensibility
- Write unit tests for core logic (spot assignment, rate calculation, ticket lifecycle).
- Use mock dependencies for external systems (DB, external APIs).
- Design for future feature additions with minimal code changes.

## 12. Documentation & Communication
- Provide class/method comments where intent isn’t obvious.
- Keep diagrams up to date with code.
- Explain trade-offs and assumptions in your design.

---
> These guidelines help structure and communicate your LLD solution clearly, making it both robust and maintainable.